WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.080
Rocket Lab is launching its 99th mission today to deliver a Japanese radar satellite that can image Earth day or night through clouds. This is Fascinating Frontiers, episode 241. Falcon hardware, cosmic spotlight, and a medical robot come after this launch. Electron is flying from New Zealand, and Space.com reports the window opens at 11.30 in the morning, eastern time. The two-stage vehicle stands 59 feet tall and has already flown 16 times this year.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:35.420
It is carrying an Earth-observing synthetic aperture radar satellite for the Japanese company IQPS.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:42.980
IQPS is on its 10th launch as it assembles a constellation of 36 SAR satellites in low-earth orbit.

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:50.390
The mission is called the Sea God Surveys. Routine landings now send Falcon 9 first stages back to flight,

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:56.910
according to Tech Bullion. Starship has demonstrated booster catches by mechanical arms at Starbase Texas.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:01:05.770
On September 28, 2026, Starship reached orbit for the first time during its 14th flight and released 26 Starlink satellites.

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:14.650
Crew Dragon has completed 21 crewed orbital flights since demo 2 in 2020, docking autonomously with the International Space Station.

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:23.010
Orbital refueling demonstrations are planned next, so a Starship lunar lander can top up propellant in Earth orbit before heading to the moon.

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:28.770
Laser communication links between Crew Dragon and Starlink satellites were tested during the Polaris

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:35.865
Dawn mission in September 2024. RCA Mons on Mars produces a water ice cloud that forms only

00:01:35.865 --> 00:01:41.265
during spring and summer mornings. Winds carry ice crystals downstream until the feature stretches

00:01:41.265 --> 00:01:47.965
more than 1,500 kilometers, according to NASA breaking. Viking Orbiter 2 from the National

00:01:47.965 --> 00:01:53.345
Aeronautics and Space Administration, or NASA, first recorded the cloud in 1977.

00:01:53.985 --> 00:01:59.905
The European Space Agency, or ESA, Mars Express spacecraft, has imaged it in detail.

00:02:00.565 --> 00:02:05.225
The thin Martian atmosphere lacks enough dust at high altitudes for normal cloud formation,

00:02:05.745 --> 00:02:08.185
so researchers are studying alternative mechanisms.

00:02:09.286 --> 00:02:14.066
Freshly formed water ice crystals stream away from the volcano and leave a tail that lasts

00:02:14.066 --> 00:02:15.306
long enough to photograph.

00:02:15.306 --> 00:02:20.426
staff. Standard nucleation around particles cannot explain those crystals, which points

00:02:20.426 --> 00:02:25.986
toward local temperature drops or wave dynamics. The same processes may operate in Earth's

00:02:25.986 --> 00:02:30.506
thinner upper layers, a setting without the complicating effects of abundant aerosols.

00:02:31.086 --> 00:02:35.966
Seasonal monitoring is watching whether the cloud's length or timing is shifting as Mars moves

00:02:35.966 --> 00:02:41.400
through longer-term climate cycles. Hold a teaspoon at the height these ice clouds appear

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:47.560
and fill it with Martian air. The whole Martian atmosphere is about 1% the density of Earth's,

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:52.760
so few molecules sit in that spoon that an Earth barometer would hardly notice the same volume.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:57.480
Ice still forms anyway. Water vapor leaves the ground near the volcano.

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:02.620
The thin air cools that vapor fast. The vapor condenses into ice crystals.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:05.880
Winds take those crystals and draw them out into a tail.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:09.660
That tail runs longer than the distance from New York to Chicago.

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:14.880
very little material is doing the work. A thick blanket of gas is not the requirement.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:20.520
A temperature that crosses a precise threshold is. That crossing happens again each year.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:25.900
Cloud formation here tracks those temperature thresholds more than the total amount of gas present.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:30.220
Wave-driven cooling might start ice at other longitudes as well.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:36.180
The volcano's topography might instead create a unique trigger with no counterpart elsewhere on the planet.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:46.731
Google and NASA are building a robot to help astronauts manage medical equipment and perform routine tasks on future moon and Mars missions, the Hans India reports.

00:03:47.411 --> 00:03:52.451
The system builds on the crew medical officer digital assistant evaluated since 2025.

00:03:53.251 --> 00:03:58.991
Spatial awareness and vision language action models let the robot read its surroundings and follow voice commands.

00:03:58.991 --> 00:04:05.631
In early tests, the open-source Pi-Zero model achieved an 85% success rate on pick-and-place

00:04:05.631 --> 00:04:07.911
tasks using Google Cloud infrastructure.

00:04:08.231 --> 00:04:12.951
The robot could retrieve an ultrasound machine for a crew medical officer during a kidney

00:04:12.951 --> 00:04:13.871
stone emergency.

00:04:14.311 --> 00:04:19.851
The project involves Google Public Sector, NASA's Human Research Program, the Johnson Space

00:04:19.851 --> 00:04:23.931
Center Robotics Team, and the Jet Propulsion Laboratory, or JPL.

00:04:23.931 --> 00:04:30.986
A shipment of Roybus seeds left South Africa this week for preparation in Texas, the newspaper reports.

00:04:31.406 --> 00:04:36.606
The cargo holds 17,346 seeds weighing 74.2 grams.

00:04:37.366 --> 00:04:45.766
The seeds are divided into untreated, scarified, and coated sets so researchers can compare performance after exposure to microgravity and radiation.

00:04:45.766 --> 00:04:50.986
The Nanolab containing the seeds will launch aboard a Cygnus spacecraft between December

00:04:50.986 --> 00:04:53.586
2026 and January 2027.

00:04:54.446 --> 00:04:58.506
This is the first indigenous South African plant seed to fly for this type of research.

00:04:59.246 --> 00:05:04.606
After four to six weeks in orbit, the seeds return for an 18-month agricultural study with

00:05:04.606 --> 00:05:06.246
students in the Cedarburg region.

00:05:07.229 --> 00:05:12.509
Virgin Galactic has named its new spacecraft Aperon after a public vote that drew responses

00:05:12.509 --> 00:05:17.509
from more than 80 countries. Laté luxury news reports. In ancient Greek,

00:05:17.829 --> 00:05:22.089
Aperon means the boundless, and the company chose it to reflect exploration.

00:05:22.649 --> 00:05:26.169
The vehicle flew to spaceport America in New Mexico on Thursday,

00:05:26.829 --> 00:05:32.609
made it to its mothership Eve. Engineers will now complete avionic software validation before flight

00:05:32.609 --> 00:05:37.749
